\input luamplib.sty

\def\title{Equipopulated States, Revisited}

@s Option int
@s Graph int
@s Vertex int
@s Builder int

@* Introduction.
In November 2026 Shisheng Li found that the left-hand partition of
7.2.2.1--(116), which divides the 48 contiguous United States into eight
connected clusters of nearly equal population, was not the optimum it was
said to be. Knuth traced the trouble to the pruning of exercise~242: the
options for that problem include two exceptional ones, New York with New
England and possibly New Jersey, whose populations lie outside $[37\,000\,000
\,.\,.\,39\,000\,000)$; and an option must not be rejected for stranding a
component whose population is not a sum of numbers in that range, if one of
the exceptional options might help to fill it. He reworked pages 115 and~116,
and asked whether the four partitions printed in answer~246, which minimize
exterior and interior costs instead of squared populations, survive the
change of options.

This program answers that question, sharing nothing with Knuth's programs but
the graph file \.{contiguous-usa-48.gb}. It reads the graph with the \GO/
port of the Stanford GraphBase, makes the options with an enumeration of its
own, attaches the three kinds of cost, and finds the cheapest covers with the
branch-and-bound |Minimize| of this repository, asking for the |k| cheapest so
that ties show up. It solves six problems in all: the four of answer~246
with the corrected options, and the two left-hand ones again with the options
as they were in 2018.

The squared-population problems of page~116 are not among them. Their costs
run to $10^{15}$, and |Minimize| keeps the cost of an option in an |int32|;
I checked those two with Knuth's own \.{DLX5} instead.

@c
package main

import (
	"bufio"
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"math"
	"math/bits"
	"os"
	"sort"
	"strings"

	cells "github.com/sjnam/dancing-cells"
	"github.com/sjnam/go-sgb/gbsave"
)

@<Declarations@>
@<Functions@>

func main() {
	@<Read the command line@>
	@<Restore the graph of the 48 states@>
	@<Solve each problem and compare it with the book@>
	@<Write the figure, if asked@>
}

@ The flag \.{-k} says how many of the cheapest covers to find; twenty is
enough to see every tie at the top. With \.{-prune=false} no option is
pruned at all, so that every connected set in range goes to the solver; that
is slower, but it makes the answers independent of any argument about which
options are useless. The flag \.{-mp} names a file to receive the
MetaPost source of the pictures below.

@<Read the command line@>=
gfile := flag.String("g", "contiguous-usa-48.gb", "the graph")
kbest := flag.Int("k", 20, "how many of the cheapest covers to find")
prune := flag.Bool("prune", true, "prune the options as exercise 242 does")
mpfile := flag.String("mp", "", "write the pictures to this MetaPost file")
flag.Parse()

@* The graph.
The graph has 48 vertices, so a set of states fits in a |uint64|. Vertex~|v|
gets the bit |1<<v|, in the order of the file, which is alphabetical by the
states' full names. Knuth's programs keep the population in utility field
|v.I| of each vertex, and the file says so: its utility types begin
\.{SI}, so field~|U| holds a string (the full name) and field~|V| an integer.

@<Declarations@>=
var (
	n     int              // the number of vertices
	name  []string         // their two-letter names
	pop   []int64          // their populations in 2010
	adj   []uint64         // the neighbors of each vertex, as a set
	index = map[string]int{} // inverse of |name|
)

@ @<Restore the graph of the 48 states@>=
g, err := gbsave.RestoreGraph(*gfile)
if err != nil {
	fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "I can't restore the graph:", err)
	os.Exit(1)
}
n = int(g.N)
name, pop, adj = make([]string, n), make([]int64, n), make([]uint64, n)
for v := 0; v < n; v++ {
	name[v], pop[v] = g.Vertices[v].Name, g.Vertices[v].V.I
	index[name[v]] = v
}
for v := 0; v < n; v++ {
	for a := g.Vertices[v].Arcs; a != nil; a = a.Next {
		adj[v] |= 1 << index[a.Tip.Name]
	}
}
all := uint64(1)<<n - 1
fmt.Printf("%s: %d vertices, %d edges\n", g.ID, n, g.M/2)

@ Two small functions turn sets into numbers and names into sets.

@<Functions@>=
func weight(s uint64) (w int64) {
	for ; s != 0; s &= s - 1 {
		w += pop[bits.TrailingZeros64(s)]
	}
	return
}

func setOf(names []string) (s uint64) {
	for _, t := range names {
		s |= 1 << index[t]
	}
	return
}

@* The problems.
A problem is a range $[|lo|\,.\,.\,|hi|)$ of populations, a few options added
by hand, a kind of cost, and what the book says about it. The field |exempt|
names a vertex whose component, in the residual graph of a candidate option,
is excused from the pruning test; it is empty when nothing is excused.

@<Declarations@>=
type problem struct {
	title  string   // how the report refers to it
	lo, hi int64    // the populations allowed
	exempt string   // a vertex whose residual component escapes pruning
	extra  []string // options added by hand
	cost   string   // \.{"ext"} or \.{"int"}
	book   string   // what the book prints
	draw   bool     // whether its optimum covers appear in the picture
}

@ The left-hand problems add the two options with New York. On page~116 they
are the {\it only\/} options that may fall outside the range, so a residual
component that contains \.{NY} is the only kind that one of them can help to
fill; that is why \.{NY} is the vertex to exempt. The problems marked
``2018'' exempt nothing, which is what the old pruning did.

@<Declarations@>=
var withNY = []string{"CT MA NY RI NH VT ME", "CT MA NY RI NH VT NJ ME"}
var problems = []problem{
	{"left, exterior costs", 37e6, 39e6, "NY", withNY,
		"ext", "the printed answer has $90", true},
	{"left, interior costs", 37e6, 39e6, "NY", withNY,
		"int", "the printed answer has $176", true},
	{"left, exterior costs, options of 2018", 37e6, 39e6, "", withNY,
		"ext", "$90, as printed", false},
	{"left, interior costs, options of 2018", 37e6, 39e6, "", withNY,
		"int", "$176, as printed", false},
	{"right, exterior costs", 50.5e6, 51.5e6, "", nil,
		"ext", "$74, as printed", false},
	{"right, interior costs", 50.5e6, 51.5e6, "", nil,
		"int", "$230, as printed", false},
}

@ Every problem goes through the same four steps.

@<Solve each problem and compare it with the book@>=
var panels [][]uint64 // optimum covers to be drawn
for _, p := range problems {
	@<Make the options of problem |p|@>
	@<Attach the costs@>
	@<Find the |k| cheapest covers of problem |p|@>
	@<Report on problem |p|@>
}

@* Connected sets.
Every option except the added ones is a connected set of states whose
population lies in $[|lo|\,.\,.\,|hi|)$. Knuth's program for exercise
7.2.2--78 finds them by a backtrack over ``lexicographically smallest proofs
of reachability.'' To share nothing with it I use the other textbook method.
Each connected set~$S$ is grown from its smallest vertex~$r$. At each step
the candidates are the neighbors of the set so far that exceed~$r$ and have
not been ruled out; we try them one at a time, and once a candidate has been
tried it is ruled out for its later siblings and their descendants. A
connected set $T$ that contains the set so far is then reached in exactly one
branch, the one that picks the first candidate lying in~$T$: earlier branches
pick vertices outside~$T$, and later ones have ruled that candidate out. So
every connected set appears exactly once.

Since populations are positive, a set that has reached |hi| can't be
extended, and a candidate that would carry it there is simply ruled out.

@<Make the options of problem |p|@>=
var opts []uint64
found, pruned := 0, 0
var below uint64 // the vertices less than the root
var grow func(s, cand, out uint64, w int64)
grow = func(s, cand, out uint64, w int64) {
	if w >= p.lo {
		found++
		@<Keep |s| unless it strands a component@>
	}
	for cand != 0 {
		v := bits.TrailingZeros64(cand)
		b := uint64(1) << v
		cand &^= b
		if w+pop[v] < p.hi {
			grow(s|b, (cand|adj[v])&^(s|b)&^out&^below, out, w+pop[v])
		}
		out |= b
	}
}
for r := 0; r < n; r++ {
	below = uint64(1)<<r - 1
	if pop[r] < p.hi {
		grow(1<<r, adj[r]&^below, 0, pop[r])
	}
}
for _, o := range p.extra {
	opts = append(opts, setOf(strings.Fields(o)))
}

@ Here is the test of exercise 242. Take the residual graph, without~$S$, and
look at each of its components~$C$. Unless an added option can take part in
covering $C$, the states of~$C$ must be covered by options from the range,
so the population $d$ of~$C$ must be a sum of $k$ numbers in
$[|lo|\,.\,.\,|hi|)$ for some~$k$, that is, $k\cdot|lo|\le d\le k(|hi|-1)$.
The smallest candidate is $k=\lceil d/(|hi|-1)\rceil$, and if it fails, so
does every larger~$k$. A component is found by starting from its lowest
vertex and adding neighbors until nothing changes.

@<Keep |s| unless it strands a component@>=
ok := true
for rest := all &^ s; *prune && ok && rest != 0; {
	c := rest & -rest
	for grown := true; grown; {
		nb := uint64(0)
		for t := c; t != 0; t &= t - 1 {
			nb |= adj[bits.TrailingZeros64(t)]
		}
		grown = nb&rest&^c != 0
		c |= nb & rest
	}
	rest &^= c
	if p.exempt != "" && c&(1<<index[p.exempt]) != 0 {
		continue
	}
	d := weight(c)
	if d < (d+p.hi-2)/(p.hi-1)*p.lo {
		ok = false
	}
}
if ok {
	opts = append(opts, s)
} else {
	pruned++
}

@* Costs.
The two costs are those of answer~246. The exterior cost of a set $U$ is the number of edges from $U$
to vertices not in~$U$. The interior cost, from exercise~244, is the number
of ordered pairs of distinct vertices of~$U$ that are not adjacent, which is
$|U|(|U|-1)$ minus twice the number of edges inside~$U$. I keep the costs in
|price|, indexed like |opts|, and also by set in |priceOf|, since a cover
comes back as a list of sets.

@<Attach the costs@>=
price := make([]int64, len(opts))
priceOf := map[uint64]int64{}
for j, s := range opts {
	size, in, out := int64(bits.OnesCount64(s)), int64(0), int64(0)
	for t := s; t != 0; t &= t - 1 {
		v := bits.TrailingZeros64(t)
		in += int64(bits.OnesCount64(adj[v] & s))
		out += int64(bits.OnesCount64(adj[v] &^ s))
	}
	switch p.cost {
	case "ext":
		price[j] = out
	case "int":
		price[j] = size*(size-1) - in
	}
	priceOf[s] = price[j]
}

@* Covers.
The options go to the solver as text in the usual format: the 48 names, then
one line per option. The cost function receives the option's number, counted
from~1 in input order, so it reads |price| directly.

The solver delivers covers as a chain, each new one cheaper than the |k|th
cheapest seen so far; when the search is over, the |k| cheapest delivered are
the |k| cheapest covers of the problem.

@<Find the |k| cheapest covers of problem |p|@>=
var text strings.Builder
text.WriteString(strings.Join(name, " ") + "\n")
for _, s := range opts {
	for t := s; t != 0; t &= t - 1 {
		text.WriteString(" " + name[bits.TrailingZeros64(t)])
	}
	text.WriteString("\n")
}
xc := cells.NewXCC()
xc.Best = *kbest
res := xc.Minimize(strings.NewReader(text.String()),
	func(o int, _ cells.Option) int { return int(price[o-1]) })
type cover struct {
	cost int64
	sets []uint64
}
var covers []cover
for sol := range res.Solutions {
	var c cover
	for _, opt := range sol {
		s := setOf(opt)
		c.sets, c.cost = append(c.sets, s), c.cost+priceOf[s]
	}
	@<Make sure that |c| is a partition of the 48 states@>
	covers = append(covers, c)
}
sort.SliceStable(covers, func(i, j int) bool {
	return covers[i].cost < covers[j].cost
})
if len(covers) > *kbest {
	covers = covers[:*kbest]
}

@ A solver is a program too, so I don't take its word that a cover covers.
The sets must be disjoint and their union must be everything; and each must
be one of the options, or |priceOf| would quietly have charged nothing for
it.

This check is not idle. Its first run caught the solver of this repository
reporting covers that left a state out, such as a cover of interior cost
\$162 that never covered Montana. The engine had let a stale entry of its
stack of forced moves survive a branch that it abandoned on cost, so that a
later node branched on an item that was not the smallest and starved another
one. That has been fixed in \.{ssxcc.gw}; every cover now passes.

@<Make sure that |c| is a partition of the 48 states@>=
var union uint64
total := 0
for _, s := range c.sets {
	if _, ok := priceOf[s]; !ok {
		panic(fmt.Sprintf("the solver returned a set that is not an option: %x", s))
	}
	union |= s
	total += bits.OnesCount64(s)
}
if union != all || total != n {
	panic(fmt.Sprintf("the solver returned a cover that is not a partition: %v", sol))
}

@ The report gives the counts of options, the costs of the |k| cheapest
covers with their multiplicities, and then every cover of minimum cost, one
cluster per line, heaviest first, with the cluster's population and cost.

@<Report on problem |p|@>=
fmt.Printf("\n%s\n", p.title)
fmt.Printf("  %d connected sets in [%d..%d), %d pruned, %d+%d options\n",
	found, p.lo, p.hi, pruned, len(opts)-len(p.extra), len(p.extra))
fmt.Printf("  the %d cheapest covers:", len(covers))
for i := 0; i < len(covers); {
	j := i
	for j < len(covers) && covers[j].cost == covers[i].cost {
		j++
	}
	fmt.Printf(" $%d", covers[i].cost)
	if j-i > 1 {
		fmt.Printf(" (x%d)", j-i)
	}
	i = j
}
fmt.Printf("\n  the book: %s\n", p.book)
for _, c := range covers {
	if c.cost != covers[0].cost {
		break
	}
	sort.Slice(c.sets, func(i, j int) bool {
		return weight(c.sets[i]) > weight(c.sets[j])
	})
	fmt.Println("  an optimum cover:")
	for _, s := range c.sets {
		var b []string
		for t := s; t != 0; t &= t - 1 {
			b = append(b, name[bits.TrailingZeros64(t)])
		}
		fmt.Printf("    %-42s %6.3fM  $%d\n", strings.Join(b, " "),
			float64(weight(s))/1e6, priceOf[s])
	}
	if p.draw {
		panels = append(panels, c.sets)
	}
}

@* The pictures.
The optimum covers of the corrected left-hand problems are drawn as the book
draws them: each state at its place in the grid of 7.2.2.1--(112), with area
proportional to population; solid edges inside a cluster and dashed edges
between clusters. Here are the places, column and row, counting rows
downward.

@<Declarations@>=
var place = map[string][2]int{
	"ND": {4, 0}, "VT": {10, 0}, "NH": {11, 0}, "ME": {12, 0},
	"WA": {1, 1}, "MT": {3, 1}, "SD": {4, 1}, "MN": {5, 1}, "WI": {6, 1},
	"MI": {7, 1}, "NY": {10, 1}, "MA": {11, 1},
	"OR": {1, 2}, "ID": {2, 2}, "WY": {3, 2}, "NE": {4, 2}, "IA": {5, 2},
	"IL": {6, 2}, "IN": {7, 2}, "OH": {8, 2}, "PA": {9, 2}, "NJ": {10, 2},
	"CT": {11, 2}, "RI": {12, 2},
	"CA": {0, 3}, "NV": {1, 3}, "UT": {2, 3}, "CO": {3, 3}, "KS": {4, 3},
	"MO": {5, 3}, "KY": {7, 3}, "WV": {8, 3}, "MD": {9, 3}, "DE": {10, 3},
	"AZ": {2, 4}, "NM": {3, 4}, "OK": {4, 4}, "AR": {5, 4}, "TN": {7, 4},
	"VA": {8, 4},
	"TX": {4, 5}, "LA": {5, 5}, "MS": {6, 5}, "AL": {7, 5}, "GA": {8, 5},
	"NC": {9, 5},
	"FL": {8, 6}, "SC": {9, 6},
}

@ The panels sit side by side, 14 units apart. The program writes only
straight lines and circles, so the file holds nothing that \TeX\ or \.{GWEB}
could misread.

@<Write the figure, if asked@>=
if *mpfile != "" {
	f, err := os.Create(*mpfile)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, err)
		os.Exit(1)
	}
	w := bufio.NewWriter(f)
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "% The optimum covers of answer 7.2.2.1-246 with the corrected options.")
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "% Written by verify.go -mp; read by luamplib and by mpost.")
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "beginfig(1);")
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "  u := 11bp; color cut; cut := (.35,.35,1);")
	for k, sets := range panels {
		@<Write panel |k|, the cover |sets|@>
	}
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "endfig;")
	w.Flush()
	f.Close()
}

@ Within a panel the dashed edges go first, then the solid ones, then the
circles on top. The largest state, California, gets radius $0.6u$.

@<Write panel |k|, the cover |sets|@>=
at := func(v int) string {
	q := place[name[v]]
	return fmt.Sprintf("(%d*u,%d*u)", 14*k+q[0], -q[1])
}
cluster := make([]int, n)
for c, s := range sets {
	for t := s; t != 0; t &= t - 1 {
		cluster[bits.TrailingZeros64(t)] = c
	}
}
for pass := 0; pass < 2; pass++ {
	for u := 0; u < n; u++ {
		for t := adj[u] &^ (uint64(1)<<(u+1) - 1); t != 0; t &= t - 1 {
			v := bits.TrailingZeros64(t)
			if same := cluster[u] == cluster[v]; same && pass == 1 {
				fmt.Fprintf(w, "  draw %s--%s withpen pencircle scaled 1.2bp;\n",
					at(u), at(v))
			} else if !same && pass == 0 {
				fmt.Fprintf(w, "  draw %s--%s dashed evenly scaled .5 "+
					"withpen pencircle scaled .4bp withcolor cut;\n", at(u), at(v))
			}
		}
	}
}
big := float64(pop[index["CA"]])
for v := 0; v < n; v++ {
	r := 0.6 * math.Sqrt(float64(pop[v])/big)
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "  fill fullcircle scaled (%.3f*u) shifted %s withcolor (1,.5,0);\n",
		2*r, at(v))
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "  draw fullcircle scaled (%.3f*u) shifted %s "+
		"withpen pencircle scaled .5bp;\n", 2*r, at(v))
}

@* What came out.
I ran the program on 24 November 2026, with the default flags and again with
\.{-prune=false}. In the second run every problem keeps all of its connected
sets, so the two problems ``of 2018'' turn into the corrected ones; apart from
that, the two runs agree on everything below.

The options come out as on page~116: 291,282 connected sets in
$[37\,000\,000\,.\,.\,39\,000\,000)$, of which the corrected test prunes
257,502 and leaves 33,780; with the two New York options that makes 33,782.
The old test, which exempts nothing, leaves 31,685, the number that page~116
gives for the options that have no solution. With those 31,685 and the two New
York options, the minimum costs are \$90 (in two ways) and \$176 (in one),
which are the costs that answer~246 prints. So that is what the printed
answer was solving.

With the corrected options the minimum exterior cost drops to \$78, attained
by two covers that differ only in where Mississippi and Oklahoma go; the next
costs are \$80 (nine covers) and \$82 (nine). The minimum interior cost drops
to \$164, attained by a single cover; then come \$168 (four) and \$170 (ten).
Both optima use the heavyweight New York option, the one with New Jersey.

The right-hand problems are untouched by the correction, since they have no
exceptional options, and they come out as printed: \$74 and \$230, each
attained by a single cover, the same covers that answer~246 draws.

The picture shows, from left to right, the two covers of exterior cost \$78
and the cover of interior cost \$164.

$$\mplibcode input partitions; \endmplibcode$$

The first two still have a cluster that runs from Arizona to Minnesota, and
the cover of interior cost \$164 has one that runs from Nevada and Oregon
across Montana and North Dakota to Michigan.

@* Index.
